# What the window looks like with no search index — both halves of it.
#
# Run with --index-declined, which attaches to nothing and puts the real MftIndexClient behind a
# launcher that starts nothing and reports a declined elevation prompt. Everything below the
# launcher is genuine, so this is the path the app actually takes — first the banner nobody has
# answered yet, then the failure after someone says no to the UAC dialog.
#
#   BertBrowser.Harness.exe --script tools/ui/index-degraded.bbs --index-declined

tree .
refresh
assert-count 8

# Nothing has been asked for yet, so nothing prompted. The banner is the offer, and the status bar
# stays quiet: two places describing one situation would read as two problems.
settle
assert-banner "administrator rights"
assert-banner start
assert-indexing no-retry
shot 01-indexer-banner

# Dismissing means "not now". It is not persisted — the offer comes back next launch, so nobody can
# permanently lose the only way in.
dismiss-banner
assert-banner none
shot 02-banner-dismissed

# The app is entirely usable without it. Browsing, folder-local search, and everything that does
# not read the MFT behave exactly as they do with an index.
search report
assert-flattened
assert-count 3
clear-search
assert-not-flattened
shot 03-usable-without-index

# Pressing Start is what raises the prompt — and only then. Declining it is a failure, so now the
# status bar speaks and offers to ask again; the banner stays out of the way.
start-indexer
settle
assert-indexing "permission declined"
assert-indexing retry
assert-banner none
shot 04-index-declined

# A whole-PC search still answers — SearchService falls back to its crawl — but says the index is
# off rather than promising a background pass that is not happening.
gsearch report
settle
assert-status "the search index is off"
shot 05-global-search-degraded
